6050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1031 "The Domain of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1033 1899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "77b779f9-f0f6-53e5-821f-44c6d9f4ce47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194453 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d001a817-7196-53c1-ab12-d39da924742e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194454 #edb/ref 1080 "2x + 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194454 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194454 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e53fd85-6f78-5e25-b366-232d67d5f57e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194455 #edb/ref 1080 "x - 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194455 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194455 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3da81810-94aa-53d8-8877-00a1fdc58973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194456 #edb/ref 1080 "x + 3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194456 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194456 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52c90e09-18bc-5734-820c-20d3653b2c7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194457 #edb/ref 1080 "2x + 3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53b8a23b-b569-5295-abd5-1c86a4ee0306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194458 #edb/ref 1080 "2x - 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194458 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194458 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7510d3fb-f51c-54ac-b082-f629a891c1c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/b7/b799b1e1fbe7819de86c131989530033d750669ddc8fee88c711b537dad982a6.png) Give your answer in matrix form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/b1/b1c2d4408c64daf16466d81c1ea97bdd32f821deb858cd09bc539fc53ebf3ad8.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/c5/c54318fa746f53209585695e2c40d80d793d2b35327b257426b4e4fa7b25463b.png)\n\nand\n\n![](images/95/952dafd201f20f1da5b0ac77527beeb4f6d7c259813dbaee74a8d6796dd5d3b2.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/fc/fc105c91cf998f6b564570697321cfb72ac30f81e88dd6c1a0decfe8495259ba.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/32/32af7a7ab867c247eff15583688e31052e2d640b1be6c6c1b1f8bd19b7b7f2f7.png) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/f2/f27bcd29776525aa543b778627e0aace4e0b0537ceac83f5750a31f5def4f762.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/c2/c281563d780c34ea5753498e0e722bfb0296fe06c5d43e82473a45901a1aa7ee.png)\n\nand\n\n![](images/2f/2fa9abbaad8230a38e1085b8842fbbd970c66addff6f388e13973ee116f0cd37.png)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/71/71175744ceb56efc01b8b18b97d6681b11b8e9b85a1d899fc9a137ab5e3c611c.png) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/0d/0d3a22cd82a02ab5122e811236d53eb4c0018e9817d2542163acc005ca5d3764.png)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/da/dad24b303457c56dc14c11b3b9de6cb827b18b8f3a03059922d1b15521ea119d.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-